# run-level-readers.allow — the adjudication half of scripts/run-level-reader-census.sh
#
# One row per file that BOTH reaches a workflow-run feed (gh run list / gh run
# view / .workflow_runs[] / actions/runs) AND reads a conclusion off it.
#
#   <path> <run-level-reference-count> <VERDICT> <reason>
#
# The count is per FILE, never a line number: line-anchored pins slide on
# insertion and red naming files the diff never touched. Adding a reference to an
# adjudicated file changes the count and reds; moving code inside it does not.
#
# VERDICTS
#   RUN-LEVEL        answers a question from the workflow-RUN conclusion. This is
#                    the hazard: continue-on-error makes a red job roll up green,
#                    so the answer is a true green about the wrong object.
#   JOB-LEVEL        the run feed is only an index; the verdict is read from jobs
#                    or check-runs, which is the honest level.
#   KNOWS-THE-CLASS  reads the run conclusion AND explicitly models the rollup.
#   HARNESS          a .test.sh; its matches are fixtures for the script it tests.
#   PROSE            the only matches are message or error strings.
#   CENSUS           this census itself: its matches are its own scan patterns and
#                    its own selftest fixtures. Adjudicated rather than excluded -
#                    an exclusion is a blind spot written into the instrument.
#
# RUN-LEVEL rows are ENUMERATED, NOT FAILED. This is a census: it establishes who
# reads what and makes that impossible to leave un-updated. Changing any reader's
# behaviour is separate work with its own row.

.github/workflows/deploy.yml 7 RUN-LEVEL anchors its path-diff base to `gh run list --status=success` (line 68); a run whose deploy job was continue-on-error red still advances the base, silently dropping that push's paths. The SIXTH reference (task-0c3069135d1b4bfd) is the `convergence` job, and it is JOB-LEVEL by construction: it reads `actions/runs/<id>/jobs` for the control-plane and instance legs' `.started_at` and NOTHING else -- a CLOCK, never a conclusion -- to date the cutoff past which a merged commit is not yet owed a deploy. There is no verdict for a run rollup to launder. It cannot regress into a run-level read either: the value is consumed as `--owed-before <ISO>`, so a `.conclusion` substituted there is not a date and the check refuses with HARNESS-UNAVAILABLE rather than passing. Note also this file carries NO continue-on-error anywhere, stated in report-deploy-failure's own comment, so the laundering shape the class is named for has no producer here. THE SEVENTH ARRIVED WITH task-103b5cc5ec4a8ccd, which gave the `convergence` job a non-zero conclusion, and it reads STATUS AND NOTHING ELSE: `gh run list --status=<st> --json databaseId,headSha,status`, once per st in queued,in_progress, asking only whether some deploy run has not yet had its turn. `conclusion` is not in the --json projection, so there is no verdict in the payload for a rollup to launder, and the field the adjudicator switches on is `status` -- a `.conclusion` substituted there matches none of queued|in_progress|waiting|requested|pending and falls to the `not in flight` arm, which makes the gate STRICTER (more likely to red) rather than greener. The direction of every possible failure of this read is toward the finding, never away from it: an unreadable list is passed as `--in-flight-source unknown`, which withholds the exit code while STILL emitting converged=false so report-convergence-failure files the issue anyway
scripts/crown-reconcile.sh 10 KNOWS-THE-CLASS the deploy-record reconciler. RE-ADJUDICATED 2026-09-06 (task-d015fb5b13973ef8) at the measured count with a reason that describes the CURRENT script — the previous row still cited a `select(.conclusion == "success")` filter over .workflow_runs[] that no longer exists (the header at :51 records its removal: THE LEGS OWN THE POPULATION — a run DELIVERED iff its control-plane or instance JOB concluded success, read from actions/runs/<id>/jobs, whatever the run's rollup says). The run conclusion IS still read, once, as `concl: (.conclusion // "none")` on the run list — and only to LABEL delivering/non-delivering runs as CANCELLED_DELIVERING / CANCELLED_NONDELIVERING in the POPULATION line (#16403); it never decides delivery, so the rollup hazard is modelled, not trusted: a run whose legs succeeded is counted delivered even when its rollup reads cancelled. The other nine references never read a conclusion: page assembly and the oldest-created_at pager stop (two, #16403 — the reads that moved the count 8->10), the dead-trigger ANY-STATUS in-window count, the `.status != "completed"` in-flight set (WRITTEN-IN-FLIGHT: declines to judge, never passes), max/min `.workflow_runs[]?.id` for existence-vs-absence ordering, the in_progress head_sha lookup, and the created_at window filter. An unreadable page is a FLOOR stated in the POPULATION line, never a pass.
scripts/registration-sample.sh 1 RUN-LEVEL reads `.workflow_runs[] | [.path, .status, .conclusion]` (line 217) and answers per-workflow status from the run rollup, not from check-runs
scripts/pds-pull-proof.sh 2 RUN-LEVEL reports the last deploy from `gh run list --json headSha,conclusion` (line 569); advisory output, but it is the run conclusion being shown as the deploy verdict
scripts/main-gate-watch.sh 2 KNOWS-THE-CLASS reads repos/<r>/actions/runs only to ENUMERATE, then asserts at check-run level; its header states the class verbatim - "`gh run list` itself launders the red (a run whose Sobelow job concluded `failure` reads `success` at the RUN level)"
scripts/release-scan.sh 5 KNOWS-THE-CLASS reads .workflow_runs[].conclusion and models the rollup explicitly, printing "its check suite rolled up green, so GitHub excluded it: continue-on-error" as a first-class case
scripts/absent-context-census.sh 11 JOB-LEVEL reads .workflow_runs[] for {id, path, status, run_attempt} only - never .conclusion - then descends to actions/runs/<id>/jobs for the verdict. Eleventh (2026-09-03, #15855, task-82059e31bcccdbd7): read_jobs_started_count/1, a second actions/runs/<id>/jobs descent that counts job rows whose .status is in_progress or completed (whitelist, -1 UNREADABLE fail-closed) to tell a queued run whose jobs NEVER started (PHANTOM, no remedy) from one whose jobs were dispatched — a jobs-level read of STATUS, never .conclusion; the verdict stays JOB-LEVEL
scripts/absent-context-census.test.sh 18 HARNESS fixtures for absent-context-census.sh. The three new references arrived with the not-yet-created-job fix: the age guard (D1C, UNDISPATCHED_YOUNG), the needs:-gated pair (D4C DISPATCHED_PENDING / D4D NAME_NOT_IN_RUN), and the both-limbs queue fixture (D10) -- four fixtures added, one removed with the old D4B in_progress mutation, net +3. Every one is a `jq` REWRITE of a captured .workflow_runs[] payload fed back through --fixtures; the fields they set are .status, .created_at, .run_attempt and .id, and NOT ONE of them reads .conclusion. The assertions that follow are on absent-context-census.sh's own class and VERDICT output, which the row above adjudicates JOB-LEVEL. So the count moves and the verdict does not -- 2026-09-01, +5 with the orphan/dormancy split (section 5c): a proved orphan, the same tuple on a LIVE head, a DISPATCHED tuple, the liveness-unreadable fail-closed arm, and the dormant-PR flip. Re-read before the count was moved: every one is the SAME shape as the rows above -- a `jq` rewrite of the captured .workflow_runs[] payload fed back through --fixtures, setting .created_at, .updated_at and .head_sha, plus new jobs-<id>.json and main-head.json fixtures. MEASURED: zero of the five read .conclusion. The assertions that follow are on the script's own class and VERDICT output, which the row above adjudicates JOB-LEVEL. So the count moves and the verdict does not. Eighteenth (2026-09-03, #15855, task-82059e31bcccdbd7): fixture 5e for the never-started phantom shape (a queued run whose job rows never left the queue) — a fixture row, not a reader.
scripts/registration-sample.test.sh 1 HARNESS fixtures for registration-sample.sh
scripts/release-scan.test.sh 4 HARNESS fixtures for release-scan.sh
scripts/stale-verdict-watch.test.sh 3 HARNESS fixtures for stale-verdict-watch.sh The third reference (2026-09-03, #15753) is section (m)'s generated GraphQL page fixture, which carries conclusion fields so the live page loop can be driven through a stub gh.
scripts/check-deploy-smoke.sh 1 PROSE the single match is an error string quoting deploy.yml's anchor
scripts/check-deployyml-filters.sh 1 PROSE the single match is an error string quoting deploy.yml's anchor
scripts/run-level-reader-census.sh 8 CENSUS its own SRC_RE/READ_RE patterns and the readers its --selftest plants; adjudicated, never excluded from its own walk
scripts/ci-measure.sh 15 JOB-LEVEL the CI measurement harness (task-ee3fc32d1615bd58): reads repos/<r>/actions/runs only to ENUMERATE run ids for a window (lines 291-305), then descends to /runs/<id>/jobs and counts per-JOB conclusions (line 149, 308-309); it answers nothing pass/fail, it tallies job-minutes and reds for the CI-diet report. Lines 330-331 are fixture rows for its own selftest. Seventh reference (2026-09-03, #15797): enrich_prior's corrected prior-attempt read (arm v1d) — still per-job, still a tally. Eight more (2026-09-03, #15845, task-3f0fff73fdf57a2d): the --breaker mode enumerates PR runs per day and per breaker job through actions/runs and reads each Decide step's verdict off the job LOG (INHERITED-FROM-MAIN or not) to COUNT inherited reds before/after — a tally for the 09-06 report on task-e638b950726fea51, never a pass/fail answer; its selftest arms b1-b9 carry fixture rows of the same shape.
scripts/main-red-breaker.sh 2 JOB-LEVEL the main-red circuit breaker (#15740): reads repos/<r>/actions/workflows/<wf>/runs?branch=main&status=completed only as an INDEX of candidate main run ids, then descends to /runs/<id>/jobs and compares per-STEP conclusions of the same-named job with this job's own step outcomes. TWO /runs/<id>/jobs reads since #16371 (M5, task-e65c78b1cd214237): one per walk-back candidate until a run is found in which the job actually EXECUTED, and the fallback read of the newest informative run when none did. Both are jobs reads; a run conclusion is only ever used to skip cancelled/skipped runs, never to answer. An unreadable main makes it LESS forgiving (exit 1). Count 1->2 adjudicated 2026-09-06 (task-ca0ae421efa0d919) after pr-meta reddened on main.

.github/workflows/elixir-main-red-attribution.yml 2 KNOWS-THE-CLASS reads `github.event.workflow_run.conclusion == 'failure'`, but ONLY as the trigger that decides whether to investigate at all -- it never answers a pass/fail question from the rollup. The verdict it publishes is computed from the failed steps' LOG (`gh run view --log-failed`, the second reference), which is job-level evidence by construction: a step that did not run contributes no ExUnit locator, and a run rollup cannot manufacture one. THE ROLLUP HAZARD IS INVERTED HERE, and that is why this is not RUN-LEVEL. The class's danger is a true green about the wrong object; this file's exposure is the opposite -- an upstream run laundered to `success` over a red job would leave this watcher SILENT, which loses an attribution rather than publishing a false one. Nothing merges on its output and no check-run it renders is required. The laundering shape also has no producer upstream: elixir.yml carries NO job-level `continue-on-error` anywhere (verified by scanning its job bodies, 2026-09-06), and its `elixir-gate` aggregator is fail-closed over an explicit allow-set of upstream results, so an elixir run that rolls up `success` with a failed job is not a shape that file can currently emit. The residual is stated in the workflow's own header rather than papered over: a CANCELLED elixir run (the pending-queue collapse elixir.yml's concurrency comment documents) is neither `failure` nor an attributable red, so this watcher is deliberately silent about it -- a collapsed run measured nothing, so it cannot have measured too little
